in @prism

in @io
in @io/socket
in @fragmentation
in @fragmentation/frame
in @mirror/shard
in @mirror/runtime/gen_prism

in @spectral/portal/handshake
in @spectral/portal/codec
in @spectral/portal/stream

in @epistemologic/property/content_addressed
in @epistemologic/property/autopoietic
in @epistemologic/property/halts
in @epistemologic/property/frame_relativity

# @spectral/portal
#
# Typed transport over a content-addressed subspace. A portal is
# `@io.socket` typed against a fragmentation subspace OID, with a
# `@mirror/shard` frame on each end. One primitive; six instances
# (session, filesystem mount, BEAM connection, cross-system
# integration, communication, identity) per the
# `~/dev/systemic.engineering/practice/insights/fragmentation/
# portals.md` table.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-26-portal-as-io-socket-over-content-
# addressed-subspace.md §"The composition":
#
#   1. @io.socket          — raw transport (TCP, unix, WS)
#   2. fragmentation       — content-addressed subspace identity
#   3. @mirror/shard       — observer-relative frame each end carries
#   4. @mirror/runtime/    — the actor; the open portal IS a
#      gen_prism             gen_prism whose ticks are the stream
#
# Wire protocol (the four fragments Alex named 2026-05-25):
#
#   a. WS handshake — socket.accept() over HTTP upgrade; frame-
#      handshake between two shards.
#   b. Single @fragmentation/frame full frame — the portal's
#      `collapse`; same shape as delta-from-null, so full sync and
#      reconnect-resync use one mechanism.
#   c. Bidirectional eigenvalue stream — normalized observations in
#      [0.0, 1.0] of the shared spectral state; bandwidth-minimal;
#      content-addressable; replayable.
#   d. Autopoietic close — halts when Reflection picks settle(self)
#      OR the host shard's @epistemologic/silicon/compute_bound.
#      max_reductions exhausts (per @epistemologic/property/halts
#      from #74).
#
# Substrate decision (Alex 2026-05-26): the wire's middle step is a
# `@fragmentation/frame` full-frame send, NOT git-patch. See
# `boot/std/fragmentation/frame.mirror`.
#
# Four fields, three actions, four properties. Mirrors @peer's five-
# axis pattern with four — the portal isn't itself an identity
# manifold; it's the bridge between two.
#
# Insight: docs/insights/2026-05-26-portal-as-io-socket-over-
#          content-addressed-subspace.md
# Namespace placement: docs/insights/2026-05-25-spectral-namespace-
#                       architecture.md (open, Apache-2.0; between
#                       @spectral/mosaic and @spectral/db)
# Corpus prior art: ~/dev/systemic.engineering/practice/insights/
#                   fragmentation/portals.md (Alex + Reed + Mara,
#                   2026-03-24)

grammar @spectral/portal {

  # ── the portal record ──────────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # Four fields. One per substrate layer the portal composes.
  #
  #   socket   -> the raw transport underneath. @io/socket.connection
  #               gives the duplex byte stream (TCP / unix / WS);
  #               the portal types it against the subspace + frame
  #               above. Re-typed from @io.socket (the lower
  #               boot/02a-io.mirror altitude) to @io/socket.
  #               connection (the connection-oriented handle from
  #               #81) as #78's body-fill landing: connection IS
  #               what the sibling grammars (handshake, codec,
  #               stream) already operate on; the re-type collapses
  #               two carrier types into one without breaking any
  #               consumer (none of the portals.md instantiations
  #               have landed yet).
  #   subspace -> the content-addressed identity. shift(oid,
  #               fragmentation): the OID of the fragmentation
  #               subspace this portal points at. The classical bit
  #               per portals.md's "teleportation" framing —
  #               receiver does socket.recv() |> @fragmentation.
  #               observe and the state was never moved, only the
  #               address.
  #   frame    -> this end's observer frame. shift(oid, shard): the
  #               OID of @mirror/shard.self() at the time the portal
  #               opened. Comparing observations across portals
  #               walks the shard parent chain per @time.convert.
  #   actor    -> the connection's gen_prism. shift(oid, gen_prism):
  #               each tick of this gen_prism is one bidirectional
  #               update across the wire. The open portal IS this
  #               gen_prism; autopoietic close fires when
  #               Reflection picks settle(self).
  type portal = {
    socket:   @io/socket.connection,
    subspace: shift(oid, fragmentation),
    frame:    shift(oid, shard),
    actor:    shift(oid, gen_prism),
  }

  # ── new: construct a portal from the four computed pieces ──────
  #
  # Build a portal record from the four field values once each is
  # computed by open's wire-protocol stages. The constructor pins
  # the design call open's body comment parked: the `subspace` field
  # is the destination state OID of the initial @fragmentation/frame
  # the handshake exchanges (f.to), because f.to IS the
  # content-addressed identity the two ends just agreed to share.
  # Same shape `@fragmentation/frame.apply(state, f) -> f.to` makes
  # explicit on the receive side.
  #
  # Field derivations (named here so open's body composes cleanly
  # against them, and so future portal sites — server-side accept,
  # reconnect-resync, in-memory test portals — share one
  # construction path):
  #
  #   socket   <- the connection produced by @io/network.connect
  #               (or @io/network.accept on the listening side).
  #               Typed @io/socket.connection per the re-type that
  #               landed with this body-fill tick.
  #   subspace <- f.to, the destination state OID of the initial
  #               frame. The handshake's frame exchange IS the
  #               subspace negotiation; both ends acknowledge f.to
  #               and store it as the portal's typed identity.
  #   frame    <- @mirror/shard.self() at open time, crystallized
  #               to shift(oid, shard). Each end carries its own.
  #   actor    <- @mirror/runtime/gen_prism.spawn with autopoietic
  #               name (the existing peer.spawn pattern:
  #               crystallize(initial_state) produces the
  #               shift(oid, gen_prism) self-reference; Fate
  #               resolves the fixed point at construction time per
  #               #69's Banach contraction argument).
  #
  # Total at the signature level — record construction never fails;
  # the imperfect surfaces ride on the caller's compositions (the
  # connect/spawn/frame primitives that produce the field values).
  new(socket:   @io/socket.connection,
      subspace: shift(oid, fragmentation),
      frame:    shift(oid, shard),
      actor:    shift(oid, gen_prism)) -> portal {
    portal {
      socket:   socket,
      subspace: subspace,
      frame:    frame,
      actor:    actor,
    }
  }

  # ── open: handshake + frame-negotiate + initial frame + stream ──
  #
  # Open a portal to a remote URI from this end's shard frame. The
  # body runs the four-stage wire protocol (WS handshake → frame-
  # negotiate → @fragmentation/frame initial → stream ticks); each
  # stage may fail, so the result is imperfect.
  #
  # `remote: ~uri` uses the existing @io.uri sigil per boot/02a-io.
  # mirror; `frame: shard` is the local observer's shard — typically
  # @mirror/shard.self(). Body composes the four wire stages from
  # the @spectral/portal/{handshake,codec,stream} sub-grammars; each
  # sub-grammar action is itself `\`-bodied where substrate gaps
  # remain (text<->bytes UTF-8 codec for handshake; oid<->bytes for
  # codec; bounded-iteration for stream). The composition at this
  # altitude is intact; Fate dispatches the leaves at runtime via
  # @mirror/serve.
  #
  # ── substrate landing (2026-05-26): the three #78 gaps closed ───
  #
  # The three primitives the previous gap-comment named as missing
  # have landed:
  #
  #   * @io/uri.{host, port, path, address}  (#78 gap 1)
  #   * @spectral/portal.new                  (#78 gap 2)
  #   * @mirror/runtime/gen_prism.            (#78 gap 3)
  #       spawn_autopoietic
  #
  # The four-stage composition is fully namable through these
  # primitives plus the sibling grammars:
  #
  #   1. address  <- @io/uri.address(remote)
  #      socket   <- @io/network.connect(address)
  #   2. host     <- @io/uri.host(remote)
  #      path     <- @io/uri.path(remote)
  #      key      <- @spectral/portal/handshake.send_upgrade(
  #                    socket, host, path)
  #      _        <- @spectral/portal/handshake.read_response(
  #                    socket, key)
  #   3. f        <- @spectral/portal/codec.read_frame(socket)
  #   4. actor    <- @mirror/runtime/gen_prism.spawn_autopoietic(
  #                    @mirror/spectral.crystallize(f.to))
  #      portal   <- @spectral/portal.new(
  #                    socket, f.to, @mirror/spectral.crystallize(
  #                    frame), actor)
  #
  # ── what keeps the body as `\` (remaining design call) ─────────
  #
  # The four-stage pipeline is sequential: each stage's output is
  # the next stage's input, and several stages have multiple
  # outputs that downstream stages re-use (`socket` flows through
  # stages 1-3; `f.to` flows through stages 3-4 as both `subspace`
  # AND `actor`'s seed). Expressing this as one expression requires
  # let-binding / do-notation at the body altitude — a substrate
  # facility mirror does not yet expose. Today the closest pattern
  # is `match (expr) { $name => ... }` (per @mirror/reload.tick),
  # which nests linearly and grows quadratically with stage count;
  # for the seven-stage pipeline above this would produce ~70 lines
  # of nested matches that read worse than the current `\`.
  #
  # Decision: keep body as `\` pending the let-binding / do-notation
  # design call. The composition is NAMED above (substrate-pull at
  # full strictness; every step is a typed primitive); when the
  # binding-syntax tick lands, this body fills in one line per
  # stage and the body-shape comment above translates verbatim. The
  # primitives are all in place; only the syntax to compose them
  # cleanly is missing. That's the design call to park.
  #
  # Halts and autopoietic remain load-bearing without the body — the
  # properties dispatch on the type-level claims, not on the body's
  # form.
  open(remote: ~uri, frame: shard) -> imperfect(portal) { \ }

  # ── tick: one round-trip across the portal ──────────────────────
  #
  # One bidirectional update on the open portal. The portal's actor
  # gen_prism applies (state, message) -> tick_result; the new state
  # advances the gen_prism's HEAD; emissions ride the eigenvalue
  # stream over the wire. Same `tick_result` shape @mirror/runtime/
  # gen_prism.tick produces — portal ticks ARE gen_prism ticks at
  # the wire altitude.
  #
  # Body composes @mirror/runtime/gen_prism.tick on the actor's
  # head crystal. The wire-side emissions encoder is implicit at
  # this altitude: the returned tick_result.emissions list is what
  # @spectral/portal/stream.write_value serializes onto the wire
  # at the caller's altitude (one f64 per emission once normalized
  # through the eigenvalue projection). The substrate-side
  # composition here IS the actor advance; the wire serialization
  # is the stream grammar's concern, not tick's.
  #
  # The `p.actor` field carries shift(oid, gen_prism) — the actor's
  # content-addressed self-reference. gen_prism.tick takes
  # (state: oid, message): the substrate coerces shift(oid, T) ->
  # oid implicitly per the autopoietic-self-reference
  # construction. Same coercion @mirror/runtime/gen_prism.send
  # uses internally when it walks gp.head before calling tick.
  tick(p: portal, message: message) -> tick_result {
    @mirror/runtime/gen_prism.tick(p.actor, message)
  }

  # ── close: autopoietic termination ────────────────────────────────
  #
  # Close the portal. Two paths, both decidable per
  # @epistemologic/property/halts:
  #
  #   (a) settlement — Reflection picks settle(self); the actor's
  #       Lawvere fixed point is reached; the gen_prism crystallizes
  #       a final state and the wire closes cleanly.
  #   (b) budget exhaustion — the host shard's compute_bound.
  #       max_reductions runs out; the substrate forces termination;
  #       any partial state is crystallized; the wire closes with a
  #       structured-reason payload.
  #
  # Returns imperfect because the wire close itself may fail (peer
  # gone, socket already broken); diagnostics ride the standard
  # recovery surface.
  #
  # Body composes @io.close on the portal's socket field. The
  # autopoietic settlement / budget-exhaustion disjunction from
  # @epistemologic/property/halts (#74) determines WHEN this is
  # called; the close action itself is the wire-level termination,
  # not the structural halt condition. Reflection's settle(self)
  # tick OR the host shard's compute_bound exhaustion is the
  # caller; both paths converge on this single action.
  #
  # ── substrate landing (2026-05-26): close body fills ───────────
  #
  # The previous gap on this action — `portal.socket` typed at the
  # lower @io.socket altitude while @io/socket.close takes the
  # connection-oriented handle from #81 — closes by the re-type
  # that landed in this same tick: `portal.socket` is now typed
  # @io/socket.connection, matching what @io/socket.close consumes.
  # The re-type touched no consumer because none of the portals.md
  # instantiations have landed yet; the substrate is single-source
  # of truth and the re-type was the right move.
  #
  # Body composes cleanly: project the socket field; close it.
  close(p: portal) -> imperfect {
    @io/socket.close(p.socket)
  }

  # ── properties: the math made load-bearing ───────────────────────
  #
  # All four substrate-level claims the portal makes structurally.
  # These ARE the design: the math of the composition turned into
  # type-level obligations the verifier checks at compile time.
  #
  #   content_addressed : identity IS the content hash. All four
  #                       portal fields are content-addressed
  #                       (socket -> ref; subspace/frame/actor ->
  #                       shift(oid, _)); the portal's OID is
  #                       determined by the quadruple. Foundational.
  #   autopoietic       : the connection's gen_prism is
  #                       self-referential by construction —
  #                       gen_prism's `name` field is itself
  #                       shift(oid, gen_prism), the Lawvere fixed
  #                       point exhibits at construction time per
  #                       Soto-Andrade & Varela 1984. The portal
  #                       inherits the autopoietic claim through
  #                       its `actor` field.
  #   halts             : both ends terminate by construction (#74).
  #                       Disjunction: autopoietic settlement OR
  #                       reduction-budget exhaustion. Decidable
  #                       because the language is sub-Turing;
  #                       Rice's theorem does not apply. This is
  #                       the is_copium thesis operationalized at
  #                       the transport altitude.
  #   frame_relativity  : observations are shard-relative.
  #                       Comparing the same eigenvalue measured at
  #                       two portal endpoints requires explicit
  #                       @time.convert through the shard parent
  #                       chain. PG §2.1 (timestamptz over
  #                       timestamp) at the substrate altitude.
  requires content_addressed(portal)
  requires autopoietic(portal)
  requires halts(portal)
  requires frame_relativity(portal)
}

out portal
out new
out open
out tick
out close
out @spectral/portal
